      'Hormonal control starts with enteroendocrine cells, special cells scattered in the lining. Each one has a surface that faces the gut contents and senses them, detecting chemicals such as acid, fat or amino acids, or stretching. In response, it releases a hormone into the blood, which carries it to its targets: other parts of the gut, the pancreas and gallbladder, the liver and even the brain, where some gut hormones affect appetite.',
